 ## Overview
-Derived Grid Rasterization converts an unstructured 3D volume mesh (e.g., tetrahedra, hexahedra) into a dense, regular 3D voxel grid spanning the mesh's Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB) [S1, unofficial]. This creates a structured data layout required for efficient volume ray-marching and axis-aligned slice planes on data that originally lacks a grid structure [S1, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods that may produce sparse structures or rely on different rasterization primitives, this method explicitly maps each voxel to its owning unstructured cell and local tetrahedron index, enabling direct lookup during rendering [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [3D Grid Rasterization](903-3d-grid-rasterization.md) by handling unstructured cell topology rather than simple surface mesh voxelization.
+Derived Grid Rasterization converts an unstructured 3D volume mesh (e.g., tetrahedra, hexahedra) into a dense, regular 3D voxel grid spanning the mesh's Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB) [S1, unofficial]. It maps each voxel to its owning unstructured cell and local tetrahedron index, enabling direct structured lookup for volume ray-marching and axis-aligned slice planes [S1, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods that produce sparse structures or rely on different rasterization primitives, this method explicitly stores cell connectivity per voxel for subsequent rendering [S1, unofficial].
 
